    <div class="text-body text-sm sm:text-[15px] leading-relaxed space-y-4 [&_h2]:text-ink [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:sm:text-xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-tight [&_h2]:mt-8 [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:leading-tight [&_h3]:text-ink [&_h3]:text-base [&_h3]:sm:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:tracking-tight [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_h3]:leading-tight [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-2 [&_a]:decoration-link/30 [&_a]:transition-colors [&_a:hover]:decoration-link">

        <p>You paste in a direct link to an image. Instead of a preview, you get an error or a blank result. The image opens fine when you visit the URL in a new tab, so it clearly exists. A browser-based tool can still fail here, even though your own browser succeeds. The reason comes down to a handful of server-side protections. None of them were built with you in mind. They exist to stop other websites.</p>

        <h2>CORS: The Browser's Own Restriction</h2>
        <p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cross-Origin Resource Sharing, or CORS</a>, is a browser security rule. It stops a script on one website from reading the actual bytes of a resource hosted on a different website. The only way around it is an <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> header from that resource's own server. The image host does not have to configure anything special to block you. This is just the default, safe behavior of every modern browser. A page can display an image from another domain just fine. But a script on that page cannot read its pixel data, for example to draw it onto a canvas and re-encode it, unless the header is present.</p>
        <p>This is exactly the situation PasteDownloadApp's old URL download feature ran into. Displaying the image was easy. Reading and re-encoding it is a different story. That step is what lets a tool convert an image or force a download with a specific filename. It requires CORS permission that many image hosts simply do not grant.</p>

        <h2>Hotlink Protection: A Deliberate Block</h2>
        <p>Separately from CORS, many servers check the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Referer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>Referer</code> header</a> on incoming requests. They reject any request that did not come from their own website. This is called hotlink protection. It exists to stop other sites from embedding an image directly and burning the original host's bandwidth. It is common on stock photo sites, some CDNs, and forums tired of their images showing up everywhere except their own pages. Unlike CORS, this is an intentional block. It targets exactly what a URL download tool is trying to do.</p>

        <h2>What Happens When the Direct Request Fails</h2>
        <p>Your browser could not fetch or read an image directly when one of these two protections applied. In that case, PasteDownloadApp automatically retried the request through <strong>corsproxy.io</strong>. That is a third-party proxy that fetches the image server-side and returns it in a way your browser is allowed to read. This got around CORS in most cases, because the proxy, not your browser, made the original request. It did not always get around hotlink protection. A strict Referer check could still reject the proxy's request the same way it would reject any other outside request.</p>

        <h2>When Nothing Works</h2>
        <p>A small number of sources block both the direct request and the proxy fallback. This usually comes down to one of a few things. Maybe a Referer check is strict enough to reject even the proxy. Maybe the source requires a logged-in session, or a signed URL that expires almost immediately. Or the host simply blocks known proxy IP ranges outright. The most reliable option in these cases does not depend on any of these protections at all. Open the image in a new browser tab and use your browser's own "Save image as" option. A real browser navigation carries the correct referrer and cookies automatically, so it gets through. If you then need to convert that saved file to a different format, drag it into PasteDownloadApp instead of pasting a URL. That is the same drag-and-drop path covered in <a href="/blog/save-clipboard-image-without-software/">saving clipboard images without extra software</a>.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Is this a bug in PasteDownloadApp?</h3>
        <p>No. CORS and hotlink protection are enforced by the image's own server and by browser security rules. Those rules apply to any website attempting the same thing, not just this tool. The proxy fallback existed specifically to work around the more common of the two. A properly configured hotlink block, though, is designed to survive exactly that kind of workaround.</p>

        <h3>Why does the image show a preview but then fail to download?</h3>
        <p>A plain <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag can often display an image that CORS still blocks a script from reading. Displaying and re-encoding are different operations with different permission requirements. That is why it was possible to see a preview and still hit a failure at the download step.</p>

        <h3>Can I fix this myself if I own the image's server?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Adding an <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> header for the domains you want to support resolves the CORS side. Loosening or removing a Referer check resolves hotlink protection. That second fix, though, reopens the exact bandwidth concern the protection was meant to solve.</p>

